/* Sample spoken answers: polished scripts a strong candidate could say, per step.
   Bold marks phrases worth reusing. open = playback + clarifying questions. */
window.CASE_SCRIPTS = {
diapers:{
 open:"<p>So, <b>to make sure I understand</b>: our client is an international hygiene products company with no presence in Saudi Arabia, considering a local factory. <b>The first question is</b> how big the KSA diaper market is. A few quick questions: should I include adult diapers as well as baby diapers? Disposable only? And should I size it <b>by sales in dollars per year</b>? <b>Could I take a moment to structure my approach?</b></p>",
 steps:{
  "Approach":"<p><b>I'll size this top-down, by age.</b> Start with the population and divide it by life expectancy to get the number of people at each age. Diapers are used by two groups: babies up to about three, and some elderly people. For each group, I'll estimate the share that uses diapers and how many they use per day. That gives diapers per day, then per year, and finally I'll multiply by the price per diaper. <b>Does that approach sound reasonable?</b></p>",
  "Population":"<p><b>I'll assume</b> a population of 35 million and a life expectancy of 80. <b>To keep the math simple</b>, I'll assume people are spread evenly across ages: 35 million divided by 80 is about 437,000. <b>I'll round that to 450,000 people per year of age.</b></p>",
  "Diapers per day":"<p><b>Let me go group by group.</b> Ages 0 to 1: 450,000 babies, all using diapers, 10 a day, so 4.5 million. Ages 1 to 2: 90%, about 405,000 children at 8 a day, roughly 3.2 million. Ages 2 to 3: 80%, about 360,000 at 6 a day, about 2.2 million. For 75 to 80, <b>that's five years of age, so 2.25 million people</b>; 30% use diapers, about 675,000 at 4 a day, so 2.7 million. <b>That's about 12.6 million diapers a day.</b></p>",
  "Market value":"<p>12.6 million a day times 365 is about <b>4.6 billion diapers a year</b>. A pack of 12 at $6 is 50 cents a diaper, so <b>the market is about $2.3 billion a year</b>.</p>",
  "Sanity check":"<p><b>It feels reasonable, and if anything conservative.</b> Babies drive about 80% of the volume, so those assumptions matter most. I assumed an even age distribution, but <b>Saudi Arabia has a young population</b>, so there are probably more babies than I assumed. <b>So I'd call $2.3 billion a conservative estimate.</b></p>",
  "The factory decision":"<p>At 10% share, the client would have <b>$230 million in revenue</b>. At a 15% margin, that's about <b>$34.5 million of profit a year</b>, so a <b>payback of about 2.9 years</b>. <b>One caveat:</b> the $2.3 billion is at store prices. The client sells to retailers, who keep perhaps 25 to 30%, so <b>payback is nearer four years</b>. That's still attractive.</p>",
  "What else to check":"<p><b>I'd group what I'd check into three areas.</b> <b>Competition</b>: who leads and how loyal customers are, which tells us if 10% is realistic. <b>Go-to-market</b>: access to pharmacies, hypermarkets and online. <b>The operating environment</b>: local manufacturing incentives, Saudization, and demand trends like the birth rate. <b>A lower-risk option</b> could be to import first and build once share is proven.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend moving ahead with the factory, subject to two checks.</b> The market is large, about $2.3 billion a year and likely more given the young population, and at 10% share the $100 million factory pays back in three to four years. <b>The key risk</b> is whether a new entrant can reach 10%. <b>As next steps</b>, I'd validate that against current brands and secure distribution partners and local incentives.</p>"
 }},
hotdog:{
 open:"<p><b>Just to play it back</b>: our client owns one hot dog stand in Hamra, profitability has declined over the past few months, and this month it lost money. <b>The goal is to find the problem and fix it.</b> A few questions: what do they sell, and at what price? Is the competition seeing the same decline? And is there a profit target?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>This is a profitability problem, so I'll start from profit equals revenue minus costs.</b> On <b>revenue</b>: the price, the number of meals sold, and any other revenue streams. On <b>costs</b>: fixed costs, like the truck, and variable costs: food, utilities, rent, labor and any waste. <b>Externally</b>, Frank's is doing fine, so <b>my hypothesis is that the problem is specific to our stand</b>. I'd compute this month's profit, then compare it with the past to see what changed. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Revenue streams":"<p><b>Today it's the standard meal</b>, a hot dog and a drink. <b>Other streams could include</b> advertising on the truck, a delivery service, paid toppings, and new menu items. <b>For now I'll focus on the standard meal</b>, and keep the others for when we talk about growth.</p>",
  "Monthly revenue":"<p><b>I'd estimate</b> about 15 meals a day for a small stand. At $6 a meal, that's $90 a day, and over 30 days, <b>$2,700 a month</b>.</p>",
  "Cost structure":"<p><b>Before looking at the list</b>, I'd expect food, the chef's salary, rent, utilities, and some wasted food. The truck itself is a fixed cost, already paid. A 3× pricing strategy means the price is three times the food cost, so <b>food costs $2 a meal</b>. <b>Strictly, rent and salary are fixed each month</b>, but they're all operating costs either way.</p>",
  "This month's profit":"<p>Food is $2 times 15 meals times 30 days, $900. Adding utilities $400, rent $800, labor $500 and expired food $200, <b>costs are $2,800 a month</b>. Against $2,700 of revenue, <b>the stand loses $100 a month</b>. <b>Interestingly, the $200 of expired food is bigger than the loss itself.</b></p>",
  "Last year vs. this year":"<p><b>Last year:</b> 20 meals gave $3,600 of revenue; food was $1,200 and other costs $1,700, so <b>a profit of $700</b>. <b>So profit fell $800.</b> Lost revenue explains $900 of it, partly offset by $300 less food bought; waste added $150 and utilities $50. <b>The main problem is volume: we sell a quarter fewer meals.</b> And waste rose because <b>the owner still buys food for 20 meals while selling 15</b>.</p>",
  "Why customers left":"<p><b>I'd split the possible causes into internal and external.</b> Internal: quality, service speed, opening hours. External: a competitor's move, less foot traffic, the economy. <b>The survey answers it</b>: 45% left for Frank's $5 student combo with fries, and 25% for our wait times. <b>So 70% of the reasons are about value and service.</b> <b>So what:</b> the market didn't change, the competitor did.</p>",
  "Increasing profitability":"<p><b>I'd split this into increasing revenues and decreasing costs.</b> <b>Revenues</b>: raise the price, carefully given price elasticity; grow volume with marketing or new menu items; and add new streams like advertising on the truck. <b>Costs</b>: a cheaper location, though location is key for us; cheaper ingredients without hurting quality; and <b>most importantly, inventory</b>. We throw away $200 of food a month, so <b>forecasting demand from monthly trends</b> would fix our loss on its own.</p>",
  "Should they cut the price?":"<p>At $5, we keep $3 per meal instead of $4. Monthly costs to cover are $1,750, so <b>breakeven is about 583 meals a month, roughly 19 to 20 a day</b>. <b>That means winning back almost every lost customer just to break even.</b> At $6 with waste fixed, we break even at about 15 a day. <b>So I'd match Frank's on value, with a combo, rather than on price.</b></p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>The stand loses about $100 a month because it lost five meals a day to Frank's new student combo, and kept over-ordering food.</b> I recommend three actions. One, <b>forecast demand and order less</b>, recovering about $150 a month immediately. Two, <b>launch a value combo at $6 rather than cutting the price</b>. Three, <b>speed up lunch service.</b> <b>As a next step</b>, track daily sales and waste for a month.</p>"
 }},
concert:{
 open:"<p><b>To recap</b>: our client is an entertainment company that wants to host a one-night concert in Riyadh with a famous DJ, and needs to decide <b>whether it's a good idea</b> within two weeks. A few questions: <b>how do they define success</b>, is there a profit target? What's the venue capacity? And do we have data from their past events?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd look at three areas.</b> First, <b>profitability</b>: revenues from tickets, sponsors and extras, minus fixed costs like the DJ and venue and variable costs like commissions. Second, <b>demand</b>: how many tickets will realistically sell. Third, <b>feasibility</b>: the license, local regulations and cultural fit. <b>I'd then compare expected profit with their $10,000 threshold.</b> <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Revenue streams":"<p><b>The core streams are tickets, by tier, and sponsorships.</b> On top of that, events can earn from <b>food carts and non-alcoholic drinks, merchandise like glow sticks and t-shirts, DJ memorabilia, and valet parking</b>. I'd start with tickets and sponsors, and keep the extras as upside.</p>",
  "Full-house revenue":"<p>VIP is 400 tickets at $50, so $20,000. Regular is 600 at $20, so $12,000. Sponsors add $6,000. <b>Total revenue at a full house is $38,000.</b></p>",
  "Costs and profit":"<p><b>I'll split costs into fixed and variable.</b> Fixed: DJ $6,000, venue $5,000, maintenance and insurance $4,000, operations $4,200, so $19,200. Variable: $3 commission on 1,000 tickets, $3,000. Total costs $22,200, <b>so profit is $15,800</b>, <b>above the $10,000 bar if it sells out.</b></p>",
  "Realistic sales":"<p><b>Using past sell-through:</b> 280 VIP tickets bring $14,000 and 540 regular $10,800; with sponsors, revenue is $30,800. Costs are $19,200 plus $2,460 of commission, $21,660. <b>Expected profit is about $9,100, roughly $860 short of the target.</b> <b>So the full-house case is misleading.</b></p>",
  "Important aspects":"<p><b>I'd group them into four areas.</b> <b>One, barriers to entry</b>: the license to organize concerts, and local business regulations, which may require a local partner. <b>Two, culture and preferences</b>: designing the event to respect local norms. <b>Three, selling out</b>: online box office, local stores and social media, starting sales one to six months ahead. <b>Four, additional revenue streams</b>: accessories, memorabilia, food carts, drinks and valet parking.</p>",
  "Closing the gap":"<p><b>The gap is small, under $1,000.</b> The easiest lever is the extra revenue streams: <b>500 people spending $5 at a food stand with a 50% margin is $1,250</b>, which closes it alone. Other options: one more sponsor, an early-bird presale to lift VIP sales, or negotiating the commission or venue fee.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend going ahead, but only once extra income is secured.</b> A full house makes $15,800, but based on past events the realistic profit is about $9,100, just under their $10,000 minimum. <b>One food concession or one more sponsor closes that gap.</b> <b>Next steps:</b> secure the license, design the event around local norms, and start ticket sales early.</p>"
 }},
banker:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me make sure I have it</b>: our client made their fortune in real estate, has up to $25 million, and is considering entering Saudi banking. <b>The questions are whether to do it, and what to consider.</b> Could I ask: do they have banking experience? Are there regulations in the way? And why banking?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd look at three areas.</b> <b>Competitors</b>: who they are, how established they are in KSA, whether they share our client's goals, and market trends. <b>Barriers to entry</b>: whether we need a Saudi partner for Saudization, and government regulations. <b>Market size and share</b>: what share our client could realistically win, and competitors' shares. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "The market":"<p><b>Two takeaways.</b> The market is attractive: profits are growing about 6% a year. But it's <b>highly concentrated</b>, with the top five holding 75% of deposits, so winning share from scratch is hard. <b>The opportunity is in faster-growing niches</b> like digital banking and small-business lending.</p>",
  "Ways to enter":"<p><b>There are two main ways in.</b> One, <b>build</b>: open a new bank from scratch. Two, <b>buy</b>: acquire an existing player. A third, softer option would be a partnership. <b>I'd compare the first two on profitability and risk.</b></p>",
  "Profitability of each":"<p><b>Option 1</b> earns 22% of $10 million, $2.2 million a year, so 15 divided by 2.2 is <b>about 6.8 years</b>. <b>Option 2</b> is $20 million for $2.5 million a year, <b>8 years</b>. <b>On payback alone, building looks faster.</b></p>",
  "Risk of each":"<p><b>Not necessarily.</b> Building is riskier: the client has no banking experience and no credibility, and <b>the $10 million is a forecast, not money that's actually there</b>. Buying gives an <b>established customer base and real profits</b>, so the risk is lower. <b>So the answer depends on the client's risk appetite.</b></p>",
  "Launch delay":"<p><b>Yes, it does.</b> The new bank earns nothing for two years, so its real payback is 6.8 plus 2, <b>about 8.8 years</b>, slower than buying at 8. And hiring 300 staff for Saudization adds cost. <b>So buying is now both faster and lower risk.</b></p>",
  "The catch":"<p><b>Yes, one significant concern.</b> 35% of the target's loans are to real estate. <b>The client's goal was to diversify away from real estate</b>, so this doubles down on it: a property downturn would hit their wealth and the bank at once. <b>I'd make the deal conditional on due diligence</b>, and a plan to reduce that share.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>Given the client prefers to avoid big risks, I recommend entering by acquiring the existing bank.</b> Three reasons: it comes with customers and real profits, not forecasts; it's already Saudization-compliant; and once the two-year launch is counted, it pays back faster, 8 years versus almost 9. <b>The condition</b> is reducing the 35% real-estate exposure. <b>Next steps:</b> loan-book due diligence, a valuation check, and retaining management.</p>"
 }},
tv:{
 open:"<p><b>So, to recap</b>: a Dubai family business with restaurants and a bus company wants to acquire a TV channel, and <b>wants a payback in under five years.</b> A couple of questions: how does the channel make money? Do they have the expertise and money? And are there regulatory barriers?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd look at three areas.</b> <b>The client</b>: financial capability, expertise, and any conflicts of interest with their other businesses. <b>The market</b>: competitors, market leaders, our potential share, and barriers such as regulations or antitrust. <b>Profitability</b>: revenue streams, prices and volume; and costs, meaning the initial investment, fixed and variable costs. <b>Since payback is the deal-breaker, I'd focus on profitability.</b> <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Revenue streams":"<p><b>A TV channel can earn from</b> advertising, subscriptions, and packages or bundles with other channels. <b>Since this channel earns only from ads</b>, subscriptions are a lever we could add later.</p>",
  "Maximum ad revenue":"<p><b>I'll go bottom-up, peak and non-peak separately.</b> Peak: $250 a minute, 10 minutes an hour, 4 hours, 300 days, so <b>$3 million</b>. Non-peak: $100, 10 minutes, 20 hours, 300 days, so <b>$6 million</b>. <b>Total: $9 million a year.</b></p>",
  "Payback":"<p>$9 million minus $6 million of costs is <b>$3 million of profit</b>. $15 million divided by $3 million is <b>exactly five years</b>. <b>That just misses their goal of less than five.</b></p>",
  "Critique the result":"<p><b>Not quite. The $9 million assumes every ad slot is sold, so it's a ceiling.</b> With actual revenue of $8 million, profit is $2 million and <b>payback is 7.5 years, well beyond the goal.</b> Peak is sold out, so peak prices may be too low, while non-peak has unsold slots.</p>",
  "Speeding up payback":"<p><b>I'd split it into increasing revenues and decreasing costs.</b> <b>Revenues</b>: charge a subscription; fill unsold ad slots through marketing, <b>advertising the family's own restaurants and buses</b>, and awareness campaigns; and host entertainment programs to grow the audience. <b>Costs</b>: negotiate a lower purchase price, reduce labor costs, and outsource or automate some jobs.</p>",
  "Synergies":"<p><b>That's a meaningful synergy.</b> The family could move about $1 million of its spending into the unsold non-peak slots. <b>It's a saving, not new money</b>, but at group level it brings profit back to about $3 million and <b>payback to five years: still not under five.</b></p>",
  "The right price":"<p><b>At $2 million of profit a year, five years of profit is $10 million.</b> So they shouldn't pay more than about $10 million, and <b>I'd keep the synergies as negotiating room.</b></p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend not buying at $15 million.</b> Real payback is 7.5 years, because the $9 million revenue is a ceiling the channel doesn't reach, and even moving the family's own ads only gets to five years. <b>I'd counter-offer around $10 million</b>, and plan to lift profit through subscriptions, filling ad slots, entertainment shows and lower labor costs. <b>Next steps:</b> test the seller's flexibility on price.</p>"
 }},
plane:{
 open:"<p><b>To make sure I understand</b>: our client, a Dubai airline, is preparing a Dubai–Paris flight on a 300-seat plane and <b>wants the ticket price that maximizes profit</b>. Questions: is it a single cabin class? Do we know how demand changes with price? And what does a flight cost?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Approach":"<p><b>This is a pricing problem, so I'll express profit as a function of the ticket price.</b> Revenue is price times seats sold, and seats depend on price, so I need a demand relationship. Costs are fixed plus variable per passenger. <b>Then I'll find the price where profit peaks</b>, and sanity-check it. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Revenue streams":"<p><b>Besides the ticket itself</b>, an airline earns from extra luggage charges, amenities like meals, Wi-Fi and seat selection, advertising on board, a companion fee for minors traveling alone, and pet fees. <b>For the ticket price question, I'll focus on the ticket</b>, and keep the rest as upside.</p>",
  "Costs":"<p><b>The main costs are</b> fuel, labor for pilots and crew, maintenance, airport fees and taxes, insurance, marketing, and the cost of food and drinks served on board.</p>",
  "Fixed vs. variable":"<p><b>Most of these are fixed per flight</b>: crew, maintenance, marketing, insurance, landing fees and most of the fuel. <b>A few grow with each passenger</b>: meals, passenger taxes, and a little extra fuel for the weight. <b>So costs equal $10,000 plus $500 times seats sold.</b></p>",
  "Demand curve":"<p>Going from $1,000 to $2,000 loses 100 seats, so the slope is minus 0.1. Plugging in 200 seats at $1,000 gives an intercept of 300. <b>So seats equal minus 0.1 times price, plus 300.</b> Checking: at $2,000, that's 100. ✓</p>",
  "Profit formula":"<p>Revenue is P times (300 minus 0.1P), which is <b>minus 0.1P squared plus 300P</b>. Costs are 10,000 plus 500 times (300 minus 0.1P), which is <b>160,000 minus 50P</b>. <b>So profit is minus 0.1P squared plus 350P minus 160,000.</b></p>",
  "Best price":"<p>Profit peaks where its derivative is zero: minus 0.2P plus 350 equals zero, so <b>P equals $1,750</b>. That sells 125 seats, for about <b>$146,000 of profit per flight</b>.</p>",
  "Sanity check":"<p><b>It makes me cautious.</b> We'd be the most expensive airline on the route with the fewest flights, and once customers compare fares our demand might be lower than the research suggests. <b>And we'd fill only 125 of 300 seats, 42%</b>, very low for an airline. <b>So the math is right, but the result is fragile.</b></p>",
  "The empty seats":"<p><b>Yes.</b> 75 seats at $900, with a $500 cost each, is <b>$30,000 more profit per flight</b>. <b>The risk is cannibalization</b>, so I'd add restrictions: group bookings only, fixed dates, no refunds. And the other revenue streams, like luggage, amenities and companion fees, add more on every seat we fill.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend a $1,750 headline fare, plus restricted discount fares to fill empty seats.</b> The headline fare maximizes single-price profit at about $146,000 per flight; a group fare adds about $30,000, and extra fees add more. <b>The main risk</b> is that we're priced above both competitors with a low load factor. <b>Next steps:</b> test demand at launch, and design fare rules that prevent cannibalization.</p>"
 }},
energy:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me recap</b>: the Ministry of Energy wants a program to <b>reduce the country's overall energy consumption</b>, ideally by 10% within five years. Consumption is growing about 5% a year and energy is heavily subsidized. Questions: is the 10% against today's level? Are there political constraints? And do we have data by sector?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd structure it in four buckets.</b> One, <b>the major consumption sectors</b>: residential, commercial and entertainment, industrial, and transportation. Two, <b>consumption reduction</b>: ways to cut use in each sector. Three, <b>feasibility</b>: engineering, expertise and available technology. Four, <b>profitability</b>: a cost-benefit analysis, including non-financial benefits like lower CO₂ and cleaner air. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Where the energy goes":"<p><b>Residential is the biggest sector at 40%</b>, and air conditioning is 70% of that. <b>So home AC alone is about 28% of national consumption</b>, the single biggest target. <b>I'd start there.</b></p>",
  "Homes, offices and venues":"<p><b>I'd use three types of levers.</b> <b>Price</b>: raise the cost of electricity per kilowatt-hour. <b>Efficiency</b>: free installation and discounts on energy-efficient appliances, and consolidating cooling systems in buildings. <b>Smarter use</b>: sensors that switch lights off in empty rooms, and removing unnecessary bulbs.</p>",
  "Size one lever":"<p>40% times 70% times 30% of homes times 30% savings is <b>about 2.5% of national consumption</b>. <b>So what:</b> one ambitious program delivers only a quarter of the target. <b>We'll need a package of measures.</b></p>",
  "Industrial sector":"<p><b>For industry</b>: raise the price per kilowatt-hour; apply higher taxes and regulation to the biggest consumers; offer free installation and discounts on efficient machines; reduce operating hours; and <b>reward companies whose R&D finds ways to cut energy use</b>.</p>",
  "Transportation sector":"<p><b>For transport</b>: raise fuel prices; make cars more expensive to push public transport; raise driving and car license fees and taxes; increase toll charges; encourage car-pooling; and <b>install charging stations for hybrid and electric cars</b>.</p>",
  "The politics":"<p><b>I'd make price changes gradual and targeted.</b> <b>Tiered pricing</b> keeps the first block of usage cheap, so ordinary households are protected, while heavy users pay more. Phase increases over several years, support low-income families directly, and <b>recycle the subsidy savings</b> into rebates for efficient appliances.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>Minister, I recommend a package, not a single measure, because no single lever gets us to 10%.</b> <b>First</b>, target home air conditioning, 28% of all energy, with efficiency standards and rebates. <b>Second</b>, gradual tiered pricing that protects ordinary households. <b>Third</b>, the industry and transport measures. <b>Next steps:</b> test each lever's feasibility and cost-benefit, size the package to reach 10%, and pilot in one city.</p>"
 }},
bank:{
 open:"<p><b>To recap</b>: our client is one of the largest retail banks in KSA, and <b>revenues have declined for two years</b>. We need to find the reason and a solution. Questions: how is revenue split across products? How have competitors done? And has anything changed in regulation?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd take an external-internal approach.</b> <b>Externally</b>: economic factors like lower disposable income, interest rates and new regulation; and market factors: are competitors facing the same issue, what do they offer, and how have market shares moved? <b>Internally</b>: our revenue streams, prices and fees, volume of customers, and product mix. <b>The mix table already hints that personal loans and cards are shrinking.</b> <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Which category?":"<p><b>Category 1, personal loans and cards</b>, and for two reasons: <b>its share fell from 55% to 50%, and the total revenue fell too</b>, so it definitely shrank in dollars. <b>For categories 2 and 3, it's undetermined</b>: they're a bigger share of a smaller total, so in dollars they could have gone up or down. <b>I'd need the actual totals to tell.</b></p>",
  "In dollars":"<p>Category 1 went from 55% of $1 billion, $550 million, to 50% of $900 million, $450 million: <b>down $100 million</b>. Car loans went from $280 to $270 million, down $10 million, and house loans from $170 to $180 million, up $10 million. <b>So Category 1 explains the entire decline.</b></p>",
  "Customers or price?":"<p><b>It's customers.</b> We lost 100,000, about 17%, while revenue per customer barely moved. <b>So this is a churn problem, not a pricing problem.</b> The next question is where they went.</p>",
  "Why are they leaving?":"<p><b>To summarize where we are</b>: revenue fell $100 million, all in personal loans and cards, because we lost 100,000 customers. <b>The survey explains why</b>: half moved to digital banks for instant approval and a better app, and 30% left over card fees. <b>So the root cause is our digital experience.</b></p>",
  "Existing or new customers?":"<p><b>There are two ways</b>: sell more to existing customers, or acquire new ones. <b>Existing customers are easier</b>: banks have high retention, and people with fixed deposits and loans can't move their accounts easily.</p>",
  "Cross-selling":"<p><b>The main lever is cross-selling</b>: offering credit cards to customers who have personal loans, and personal loans to customers who have credit cards. Other ideas are bundles, loyalty rewards and pre-approved offers.</p>",
  "Size the cross-sell":"<p>30% of 500,000 have only a loan, so 150,000, and 10% are interested in a card, so <b>15,000 new cardholders</b>. Annual fees: 15,000 times $250 is <b>$3.75 million</b>. Default charges: 5% of them default, times $20,000 of spending, times 10%, is <b>$1.5 million</b>. <b>Total: $5.25 million a year.</b></p>",
  "Total upside":"<p>Adding the $18.75 million from the other direction, <b>cross-selling brings about $24 million a year</b>. <b>That's only about a quarter of the $100 million we lost</b>, so it helps, but we also need to stop customers leaving.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend going ahead with the cross-selling strategy, worth about $24 million a year.</b> Personal loans and cards are behind the decline, and selling more to existing customers is the easiest win. <b>But it won't close the $100 million gap alone</b>, so we should also fix why customers leave for digital banks, and <b>look more deeply at car and house loans</b>, which may also be contributing. <b>Next steps:</b> identify customers at risk of leaving.</p>"
 }},
crypto:{
 open:"<p><b>Let me play that back</b>: our client is a startup that wants to create a cryptocurrency for use in Lebanon, where trust in banks is low. <b>The question is whether it's a good idea.</b> A few questions: does the client have the expertise and funding? Who are the competitors? And what does success look like for them?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>I'd look at three areas.</b> <b>The client</b>: crypto expertise and the financial capability to back the idea. <b>The market</b>: the size of crypto in Lebanon, competitors and their shares, and barriers like legislation, regulation and access to distribution. <b>Profitability</b>: revenue from fixed fees and transaction commissions, prices and expected volume; and costs: the initial investment and operating expenses. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Key factors":"<p><b>Whether someone uses crypto depends on</b> their age, income, risk appetite, tech literacy, ability to trade, and knowledge of crypto. <b>I'd segment by age first, then by income</b>, since those are the easiest to estimate.</p>",
  "How many users?":"<p>Six million people in four age groups is 1.5 million each. 10% of the youngest is 150,000; 25% of 21 to 40 is 375,000; 5% of 41 to 60 is 75,000; and none over 60. <b>That's 600,000 crypto users.</b> Bitcoin has 60%, so the untapped 40% is about <b>240,000 people. That's our maximum available market.</b></p>",
  "Transactions":"<p>High income: 24,000 people at 7 a week, 168,000. Middle: 120,000 at 3, 360,000. Low: 96,000 at 1, 96,000. <b>About 624,000 transactions a week.</b></p>",
  "Profit":"<p>The fixed charge is 50 cents times 624,000, <b>$312,000</b>. The average commission is half at 60 cents, 40% at 40 cents and 10% at 20 cents, so <b>48 cents</b>, about <b>$300,000</b>. Revenue is about $612,000 a week, and at a 10% margin, <b>profit is about $61,000 a week</b>.</p>",
  "Why only 10%?":"<p><b>The real question is why costs are so high.</b> First, a heavy <b>initial investment</b>: licenses and approvals, a site for the equipment, servers, and cooling for machines that run constantly. Second, the <b>cost of validating transactions</b> on the blockchain. Third, a high <b>customer acquisition cost</b> to win users away from Bitcoin.</p>",
  "Payback":"<p>$61,000 a week times 52 is about <b>$3.2 million a year</b>. $5 million divided by $3.2 million is <b>about 1.6 years: well within their three-year goal</b>, if we win those users.</p>",
  "Stress test":"<p><b>That's the key risk.</b> Profit per user is about $13 a year. To recover $5 million in three years we need about $1.7 million a year, so <b>about 126,000 users, more than half the market Bitcoin doesn't hold</b>. And any new competitor would shrink that 40%.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>It looks like a profitable idea, so I'd recommend launching, carefully.</b> At full potential it makes about $61,000 a week and pays back the $5 million in under two years. <b>But</b> any new competitor would shrink the 40% we assumed, and we need about 126,000 users to hit the three-year goal. <b>Next steps:</b> pilot to test adoption, work on costs to lift the 10% margin, and engage the central bank on the rules.</p>"
 }},
hair:{
 open:"<p><b>So, to recap</b>: our client has created a pill that reverses hair loss and wants to sell it in Lebanon. <b>The goal is to estimate first-year revenue.</b> Questions: how many pills does a patient need? Are there competitors? And what do the alternatives cost?</p>",
 steps:{
  "Framework":"<p><b>Revenue is price times volume, so I'd structure it in those two buckets.</b> <b>Price</b>: the price per pill, how many pills a full treatment needs, and the pricing strategy: cost-based, value-based or competitor-based. <b>Volume</b>: the market size in Lebanon and the share we could win. <b>Does that sound good?</b></p>",
  "Pricing strategy":"<p><b>I'd combine value-based and competitor-based pricing.</b> <b>Value</b>: one pill cures hair loss for life, which is worth a lot to patients. <b>Competitors</b>: transplants at $5,000 are the reference customers will compare against. <b>Cost-based pricing wouldn't capture that value</b>, since the pill is cheap to make.</p>",
  "Price":"<p><b>I'd price it at 1.5 to 2 times a transplant</b>, so <b>$7,500 to $10,000</b>, starting toward the lower end because incomes in Lebanon limit what most people can pay.</p>",
  "Who wants it":"<p><b>It's mostly men, but some women too.</b> Men: 150,000 aged 21 to 40, 375,000 aged 41 to 60, and 300,000 aged 61 to 80, so 825,000. Women: 37,500 aged 41 to 60 and 150,000 aged 61 to 80, so 187,500. <b>About 1 million people are interested.</b></p>",
  "Who can afford it":"<p><b>No, I'd segment by income.</b> High income: 100,000, all able to pay. Middle: 400,000 times 30%, 120,000. Low: none. <b>So 220,000 people can afford it.</b></p>",
  "Year-one revenue":"<p><b>Not all in year one</b>: only early adopters and innovators buy a new product right away. At 20%, that's <b>44,000 buyers times $7,500, about $330 million in year one.</b></p>",
  "Is it profitable?":"<p><b>Not necessarily: revenue isn't profit.</b> First, <b>R&D for a drug like this is very high</b>, so it may take a few years to break even. Second, <b>the price may be overestimated</b>: if results are similar to a transplant, speed and ease may not justify a 50 to 100% premium.</p>",
  "Year-one profit":"<p>Manufacturing is 44,000 times $100, $4.4 million, plus $40 million of marketing, so <b>year-one profit is about $286 million, recovering about 70% of the R&D</b>. <b>But each person buys only once</b>, so the Lebanese market shrinks after early adopters.</p>",
  "Recommendation":"<p><b>I recommend launching at around $7,500.</b> First-year revenue is about $330 million and profit about $286 million, so the R&D is recovered within about two years. <b>The main risks</b> are that the premium over a transplant may not hold, and that a one-time purchase means the market dries up. <b>Next steps:</b> test price sensitivity, plan other countries, and consider financing for middle-income buyers.</p>"
 }}
};

